<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polynesia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Polynesia is the region of the central and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-ocean">Pacific Ocean</a> within a triangle formed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Rapa Nui</a> (Easter Island), with over a thousand islands. Its peoples descend from seafarers of the Lapita culture who reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> and Samoa nearly 3,000 years ago, then spread across the eastern Pacific in long canoe voyages between about 1000 and 1300 CE, sharing related languages, chiefly ranks and marae temples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polynesia.jpg" alt="Polynesia" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hobe / Holger Behr · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_OC-Polynesia.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Polynesia has only about 300,000 to 310,000 square kilometres of land spread over a vast part of the central and southern Pacific, and more than 270,000 of them are in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. The Hawaiian archipelago makes up about half of the remainder. Most islands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a> and Samoa, are volcanic and built by hotspots, while New Zealand, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk-island">Norfolk Island</a> and Ouvéa are the unsubmerged portions of the largely sunken continent of Zealandia. The other main island groups within the triangle are Samoa, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a>, the Cook Islands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuvalu">Tuvalu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokelau">Tokelau</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niue">Niue</a>, Wallis and Futuna and French Polynesia. The French writer Charles de Brosses first used the term Polynésie in 1756 for all the islands of the Pacific, and Jules Dumont d'Urville proposed a narrower definition in 1831.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By linguistic, archaeological and genetic evidence the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> are a subset of the sea-migrating Austronesian people, whose languages trace back through Island Melanesia and Maritime Southeast Asia to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. The Express Train model, supported by most current data, holds that an expansion out of Taiwan reached Island Melanesia by roughly 1400 BCE and western Polynesia around 900 BCE, followed by a pause of about a thousand years before settlement of central and eastern Polynesia. Two other models, the Entangled Bank and the Slow Boat, stress longer interaction and admixture with peoples in Melanesia. Language, social life and material culture were distinctly Polynesian by 1000 BCE.</p><p>The sweet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potato">potato</a>, native to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, was widespread in Polynesia when Europeans arrived. Remains in the Cook Islands date to before 1400, and the present consensus is that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> who travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and back brought it to central Polynesia around 1300. Whether Polynesians reached the Americas, and with what effects, remains contentious. Thor Heyerdahl's claim in the mid-20th century that Polynesians originated in the Americas is regarded as a misconception.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Polynesia is a cultural term for one of the three parts of Oceania, with Melanesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micronesia">Micronesia</a>. It divides into West Polynesia, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tonga">Tonga</a> and Samoa, and East Polynesia, which includes Tahiti, the Marquesas, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter-island">Rapa Nui</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a>. Polynesian languages belong to the Oceanic branch of the Austronesian family and are similar to one another. The ancestral homeland, from a Proto-Nuclear Polynesian word, appears as Hawaiki in New Zealand and Havaiʻi in the Society Islands, and the largest islands of Hawaii and Samoa are called Hawaiʻi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savaii">Savaiʻi</a>. Leaders were generally chosen by hereditary bloodline, though Samoa has a system, fa'amatai, that combines heredity with practical skills.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Polynesia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under the expansionist Jules Ferry the republic acquired Indochina, Madagascar, vast territories in West and Central Africa and much of <b>Polynesia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-third-republic">French Third Republic · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The landing of the first London Missionary Society missionaries at Matavai Bay on 5 March 1797 is still a public holiday in French <b>Polynesia</b>, the Arrivée de l’Évangile.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-tahiti">Kingdom of Tahiti · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Rapa Nui, the island that Europeans named Easter Island, is a small triangle of volcanic land some 3,700 kilometres off the coast of Chile and about 2,600 kilometres from Mangareva in eastern <b>Polynesia</b>, one of the most remote inhabited places on Earth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rapa-nui">Rapa Nui · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The annexation of Easter Island, Rapa Nui, in 1888 extended Chile into <b>Polynesia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Jared Diamond popularised the image of an “express train to <b>Polynesia</b>”, a rapid migration from Taiwan with little mixing on the way, while other scholars stressed long development within Melanesia.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>…genetics show that their ancestry, as part of the wider Austronesian peoples, goes back about 5,000 years to the Indigenous peoples of Taiwan, whose descendants moved through the Philippines, along the edge of Melanesia, where they left Lapita pottery, and across <b>Polynesia</b> to New Zealand last of all.</blockquote><a href="/culture/maori">Māori · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
